WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.600
Size each worker for the models it will serve.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:11.380
GPU architecture determines supported acceleration features, while VRAM determines whether model weights, context cache, and runtime overhead fit.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.720
More GPUs do not create shared memory automatically.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:23.020
A runtime must support tensor or pipeline parallelism to divide one model across devices, and cross-device communication may become the bottleneck.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:28.600
Replicating a model on separate GPUs is often simpler when the goal is concurrent capacity.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:35.300
CPUs handle tokenization, request processing, data movement, and services that surround inference.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:41.440
System RAM must accommodate model loading, caches, and non-GPU components without swapping.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:52.700
Use fast local SSD storage for active model copies when possible; repeatedly loading large models over a shared network can delay startup and turn storage into a cluster-wide dependency.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.160
Network design follows traffic patterns.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:04.500
Small text prompts may use little bandwidth, but model distribution, multimodal inputs, logging, backups, and distributed inference can move substantial data.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.880
Measure both bandwidth and latency.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:13.820
Choose switches with enough nonblocking capacity, appropriate port speeds, and management features for segmentation and monitoring.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:22.440
A fast link cannot compensate for an oversubscribed switch, poor cabling, or a design that transfers model state unnecessarily between nodes.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:24.660
Account for physical infrastructure.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:32.880
Confirm power circuits, outlet distribution, power-supply headroom, heat removal, airflow, and noise before installing dense compute.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:39.420
Rack systems improve serviceability and cable management, while desktop systems can be economical for a small lab.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:47.720
Consumer hardware may offer strong performance per dollar but usually has fewer remote-management, redundancy, validation, and support features.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:53.220
Document those tradeoffs rather than assuming low purchase cost means low operational cost.
